    {
      "entityId": "fact.caldris.era.hearths-and-standing-stones",
      "name": "Era — Hearths and Standing Stones",
      "components": [
        {
          "qualifiedTypeId": "game.core.world.fact",
          "value": {
            "status": "active",
            "summary": "c. 3100–1900 BS. Permanent villages, managed woodland, long barrows, river shrines, and seasonal moot grounds spread across both continents. Authority was local and layered rather than royal. Many modern customary rights—common grazing, guest-right, sanctuary at a lit hearth—claim origin here. Present residues: village commons, oath stones, funeral breads, taboo groves, and land claims that predate written title. Scholars dispute whether the era's carved “lamps” depict stars, seed baskets, or something held by a dragon's claw.",
            "provenance": "Caldris world bible — seven eras",
            "visibility": "public"
          }
        },
        {
          "qualifiedTypeId": "game.core.world.knowledge.classification",
          "value": {
            "subjectKind": "event",
            "sensitivity": "open"
          }
        }
      ],
      "containment": {
        "containerEntityId": "location.caldris.atlas",
        "slot": "knowledge"
      }
    },
    {
      "entityId": "fact.caldris.era.the-crownmaking",
      "name": "Era — The Crownmaking",
      "components": [
        {
          "qualifiedTypeId": "game.core.world.fact",
          "value": {
            "status": "active",
            "summary": "1–560 AS. Modern kingdoms, marches, leagues, and city institutions consolidated. Borders shifted through marriage, river change, local consent, conquest, and clerical arbitration. This was not constant war: long local peaces alternated with short brutal conflicts. Most present capitals acquired their walls, courts, guild privileges, and civic identities in this era. Present residues: royal law, layered local rights, city charters, noble titles, border monasteries, and the common belief that each current polity is older and more inevitable than it really is.",
            "provenance": "Caldris world bible — seven eras",
            "visibility": "public"
          }
        },
        {
          "qualifiedTypeId": "game.core.world.knowledge.classification",
          "value": {
            "subjectKind": "event",
            "sensitivity": "open"
          }
        }
      ],
      "containment": {
        "containerEntityId": "location.caldris.atlas",
        "slot": "knowledge"
      }
    },
    {
      "entityId": "fact.caldris.era.the-first-footprints",
      "name": "Era — The First Footprints",
      "components": [
        {
          "qualifiedTypeId": "game.core.world.fact",
          "value": {
            "status": "active",
            "summary": "before c. 3100 BS. The oldest stories describe people following immense animals between receding ice and warm river valleys. Dragons appear in these stories as weather, mountains, judges, and neighbours, making it unclear which passages are literal. Present residues include migration songs, standing stones, seasonal rights of passage, and burial mounds placed where no modern border makes sense. Hidden possibility: several “roads of the first people” follow a pattern visible only from the air and converge on six places now occupied by dragon domains.",
            "provenance": "Caldris world bible — seven eras",
            "visibility": "public"
          }
        },
        {
          "qualifiedTypeId": "game.core.world.knowledge.classification",
          "value": {
            "subjectKind": "event",
            "sensitivity": "open"
          }
        }
      ],
      "containment": {
        "containerEntityId": "location.caldris.atlas",
        "slot": "knowledge"
      }
    },
    {
      "entityId": "fact.caldris.era.the-lantern-concord",
      "name": "Era — The Lantern Concord",
      "components": [
        {
          "qualifiedTypeId": "game.core.world.fact",
          "value": {
            "status": "active",
            "summary": "c. 840–116 BS. The Concord began as mutual protection for merchants, pilgrims, and messengers. It standardized measures, seals, road maintenance, maritime signals, debt arbitration, and the safety of certain archives. Over centuries it accumulated land, courts, armed escorts, hostage guarantees, and the power to declare whose records counted. It never formally called itself an empire. Its critics did. The Concord linked both continents and created many roads, lighthouses, script traditions, and legal concepts still used today. It also turned obligations into inheritable instruments, displaced communities whose claims could not be expressed in its forms, and learned to hide coercion inside neutral procedure. Present residues: Lantern Roads, seven-sided seals, archive houses, standard bridge widths, international guest-right, suspiciously similar court vocabulary, and dormant debt clauses.",
            "provenance": "Caldris world bible — seven eras",
            "visibility": "public"
          }
        },
        {
          "qualifiedTypeId": "game.core.world.knowledge.classification",
          "value": {
            "subjectKind": "event",
            "sensitivity": "open"
          }
        }
      ],
      "containment": {
        "containerEntityId": "location.caldris.atlas",
        "slot": "knowledge"
      }
    }
